-- title:
--   Inertia acts trivially on the reduction map of J_H
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a non-zero natural number and $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M\mathbb{Z})^{\times}$ a subgroup, and let $\Gamma_H =$ [`CohCarrier.GammaH M H`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L133) be the congruence subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})$ obtained as the image, under the inclusion $\Gamma_0(M) \hookrightarrow \mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})$, of the preimage of $H$ under the homomorphism [`CohCarrier.gamma0Units`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L121) sending $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M)$ to the unit of $\mathbb{Z}/M\mathbb{Z}$ given by the reduction of its lower-right entry. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (here `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`) with residue field $k_A$, and assume [`ModularCurve.ReductionInputsQExpModL A (CohCarrier.GammaH M H)`](def/ModularCurve_QExpReductionModL.html#L296): there exists a map of places from the places of the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the $q$-expansion function field $F_{\mathbb{Q}}(\Gamma_H) =$ `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ Γ_H` (the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by the ratios of integral forms) to the places of $F_{k_A}(\Gamma_H)$, satisfying `IsLaurentPlaceReduction` for the residue map $A \to k_A$ and satisfying `LaurentPrincipalGeneratedByIntegral`. Let $\tau$ be a $\mathbb{Q}$-automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying in `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ`, that is, in the image of the inertia subgroup of $A$ inside the decomposition subgroup, and let $z \in J_H(M) = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \cdot F(\Gamma_H)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. Then the reduction homomorphism [`ModularCurve.reductionQExpModL A (CohCarrier.GammaH M H)`](def/ModularCurve_QExpReductionModL.html#L312), the $\mathrm{Pic}^0$-level map attached to a choice of such reduction data, takes the same value on $\tau \bullet z$ (for the coefficientwise Galois action on $\mathrm{Pic}^0$) as on $z$.
--
--   This is the statement that inertia at $A$ acts trivially on the reduction of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of the Jacobian $J_H(M)$, the criterion underlying the good-reduction behaviour of the Galois action in the sense of Serre–Tate. It feeds the triviality of the Tate-module Galois representation of $J_H$ on inertia and the subsequent analysis of Frobenius, Hecke operators and the reduction kernel on the Tate module.
